Investigation: Consumers Stopped Fetching from Artemis

Date of logs: 2026-10-03 System: SMPP gateway app (Spring + JMS) consuming from Apache Artemis Status: Root cause narrowed to app shutdown/restart; trigger still to be confirmed

Summary

Consumers on the wpk-sms-send queue stopped because the application was shut down and restarted, not because of an Artemis fault. The logs do not show who or what triggered the shutdowns.

Findings
Time (3 Oct)	Event
13:07:19	Context closing - unbinding all SMPP sessions (shutdown began)
13:07:31	DefaultMessageListenerContainer: listener setup for wpk-sms-send failed with java.lang.InterruptedException
13:07:42	Application starting again
13:07:51	Application started (about 9.6 s)
13:28:37	Shut down again. No startup entry after this in root.log

Other observations:

The Artemis client logs had 0 errors, warnings or exceptions.
No AMQ error codes, slow-consumer messages, or connection-loss messages appeared.
The InterruptedException is a side effect of the listener being interrupted during shutdown.
Messages were migrated from the DB queue back to the Artemis queue between 13:03 and 13:26, which shows messages were backing up in the DB queue.
root.log has no startup after 13:28:37, but another log was still being written until 14:27, so it is unconfirmed whether the app came back up.
How the logs were analysed
Download the log archive from the server (e.g. with pscp), and unzip it.
Search the Artemis logs for errors:
bash
   grep -ciE "error|exception|warn" artemis*.log*
Look for warnings and errors in the main app log:
bash
   grep -E " ERROR | WARN " root.log | cut -c1-250 | head -30
Find the shutdown and startup events:
bash
   grep -inE "shutdown|stopping|closing|Started .* in" root.log | cut -c1-200
Check how many messages were migrated per hour:
bash
   grep "migrated" autoMigrate.log | cut -c1-13 | sort | uniq -c
Notes for Windows users
In Git Bash, use forward slashes in paths (C:/temp/), because C:\temp loses its backslash.
Linux-only commands (last, journalctl, ps -ef) must be run on the server, not locally.
Next steps
Confirm whether Tomcat was restarted or a deployment was done at about 13:07 and 13:28.
Check the Tomcat catalina.out on the server for the cause of the 13:28 shutdown (for example OutOfMemoryError or a normal stop sequence).
Confirm the application is currently running.
Consider alerting on Context closing events so unexpected restarts are noticed quickly.
